# Email Agency — production Apache config for the React SPA build.
#
# This site is built as a static single-page app (React Router handles all
# in-app navigation client-side). Apache needs two things it doesn't do by
# default: fall back to index.html for any URL that isn't a real file on
# disk (so a direct visit or refresh on e.g. /about-us doesn't 404), and
# 301 the handful of legacy WordPress URLs that had real content or real
# incoming links but no equivalent page in the rebuild.
#
# This replaces any previous WordPress .htaccess entirely — WordPress's own
# rewrite rules route everything through index.php, which no longer exists.

RewriteEngine On

# ── Legacy WordPress URLs with no equivalent route ──────────────────────────
# /solutions/ was a real hub page on the old site; the rebuild folds that
# into a section of the homepage instead of its own page. NE (noescape) is
# required here — without it Apache percent-encodes the # to %23, which
# browsers treat as a literal character in the path rather than a fragment,
# breaking the redirect (confirmed by testing against a local Apache
# instance before this shipped).
RewriteRule ^solutions/?$ /#ea-solutions [R=301,NE,L]

# /solutions/data-lists/ had real content, no matching solution in the
# rebuild — closest topical match.
RewriteRule ^solutions/data-lists/?$ /solutions/lead-generation [R=301,L]

# /mtmp/ (external Google Form short-link), /ntl-summit/ (broken stub on the
# old site, no real content), /articles/ (empty blog hub, zero posts).
RewriteRule ^mtmp/?$ / [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^ntl-summit/?$ / [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^articles/?$ / [R=301,L]

# /home was an early build's homepage route, superseded by / (the LabPage
# rebuild) and retired — 301 instead of a bare 404 in case it was ever
# indexed, bookmarked, or linked externally.
RewriteRule ^home/?$ / [R=301,L]

# WordPress/Yoast default junk URLs — theme-builder taxonomy archives, the
# default post category, the default author archive, RSS endpoints. None of
# it is real content.
RewriteRule ^category/ / [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^fusion_tb_category/ / [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^element_category/ / [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^author/ / [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^feed/?$ / [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^comments/feed/?$ / [R=301,L]

# ── SPA fallback, scoped to real routes ─────────────────────────────────────
# Only the actual routes from App.jsx get the index.html fallback (so a
# direct visit or refresh on e.g. /about-us doesn't 404). This used to be a
# blanket "anything that isn't a real file" rewrite, which meant a genuinely
# broken URL got served the SPA shell with an HTTP 200 — indistinguishable,
# to any crawler or tool that doesn't render JS (most SEO auditors, several
# non-Google bots), from a real page. NotFoundPage sets a client-side
# noindex tag, but the HTTP status itself was still wrong. Listing routes
# here instead means anything NOT in this list falls through to the real
# 404 below. / itself needs no entry — dist/index.html is a real file, so
# Apache serves it directly without hitting rewrite rules at all.
#
# /solutions/<anything> is intentionally a wildcard: the slug is validated
# client-side (SolutionDetailPage redirects an unknown slug back to
# /#ea-solutions rather than 404ing), so every /solutions/* path is a
# legitimate in-app state, not a broken URL.
RewriteRule ^(about-us|contact-us|privacy-policy|terms-conditions|phoenix-rising-foundation|lab)/?$ /index.html [L]
RewriteRule ^solutions(/[^/]+)?/?$ /index.html [L]

# ── Real 404s for everything else ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything not matched above (a typo, an old link, a bot probing random
# paths) hits Apache's normal file-not-found handling. ErrorDocument swaps
# in the SPA shell as the response body — so it still renders the site's
# own styled 404 page via NotFoundPage/React Router's catch-all route — but
# keeps the actual HTTP status as 404, not 200.
ErrorDocument 404 /index.html
